# BuildBuddy WORKFLOW RUNNER image -- the container //buildbuddy.yaml runs its steps in.
#
# Not to be confused with //docker/Dockerfile.rbe, which is the image Bazel ACTIONS execute in
# on the executors. This one hosts the `bazel` client that drives them.
#
# VERSIONED ON ITS OWN PATCH LINE (v1.0.24.x). This image and //docker/Dockerfile.rbe are the
# two halves of one Bazel run -- this hosts the client, that hosts the actions -- and they were
# tagged identically, patch included, through v1.0.24.3. The hermetic-LLVM migration then moved
# the executor to v1.0.25.1 without rebuilding this image, and that was correct: this image
# hosts the Bazel client, not the toolchain, so an executor content change says nothing about
# "built and tested together" here, and re-aligning the tags would mint a runner tag with no
# content behind it. The patch line is per image now.
#
# A bump here touches exactly two files: every container_image in //buildbuddy.yaml
# (grep 'buildbuddy-workflow-runner:' re-derives that list) and warmup_additional_images in
# //k8s/buildbuddy/values-workflows.yaml. Push the image BEFORE moving any reference.
#
#   docker buildx build --platform linux/amd64 --provenance=false --sbom=false \
#     -f docker/images/Dockerfile.workflow-runner \
#     -t registry.carverauto.dev/serviceradar/buildbuddy-workflow-runner:<tag> --push docker/images/
#
# --provenance=false --sbom=false is not optional. Without them buildx wraps the image in an
# OCI index carrying an unknown/unknown attestation manifest; every earlier tag in this
# registry family is a plain single manifest, and Harbor holds this repository immutable, so a
# mis-shaped tag cannot be repaired -- only abandoned. v1.0.24.4 was pushed that way and left
# unreferenced; v1.0.24.5 is the same layers in the established shape.
#
# Never re-push over an existing tag: the executors warm this image
# (//k8s/buildbuddy/values-workflows.yaml), so a moved tag means some executors hold the old
# layers and others the new, with nothing in git showing a change.
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS
#
# `container_image: "ubuntu-24.04"` in buildbuddy.yaml resolves to
# gcr.io/flame-public/rbe-ubuntu24-04, which is 398 MB compressed / 1.1 GB unpacked and lives
# outside the cluster. Every cold executor pays that pull across the internet before step 1.
# This image is the same Noble base, carries only what the workflow actually uses, and is
# served by Harbor in the same cluster as the executors.
#
# A Dockerfile rather than an oci_image because the contents are apt packages. The
# //docker/images/*.bzl macros assemble images from Bazel-built artifacts and pre-fetched
# .debs declared one by one in MODULE.bazel; git alone drags in a large transitive deb set,
# which would be a lot of http_file entries for an image that changes once a year.
# //docker/Dockerfile.rbe is the existing precedent for this class of image.
#
# THE `buildbuddy` USER IS LOAD-BEARING
#
# BuildBuddy runs the workflow runner as the user named in the action's `user` field, which
# defaults to `buildbuddy`. An image without it fails while building the OCI spec:
#
#   create OCI bundle: create spec: get container user:
#     lookup user "buildbuddy" in /etc/passwd: user not found
#
# That happens BEFORE an invocation exists, so nothing appears in the BuildBuddy UI -- the run
# looks like it was never triggered while the executor re-enqueues several times a second,
# forever. uid/gid 1001 and the home directory below match gcr.io/flame-public/rbe-ubuntu24-04
# exactly; do not renumber them. build:ci --disk_cache is /home/buildbuddy/bazel-disk-cache
# on this home (//k8s/buildbuddy/README.md). Dropping --create-home or moving the home makes
# the first CI Bazel invocation fail creating that directory.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT
#
#   bazel / bazelisk  BuildBuddy's ci_runner provides the Bazel client. Verified: the upstream
#                     rbe-ubuntu24-04 image has neither on PATH, and workflows on it run bazel.
#   docker            buildbuddy_setup_docker_auth.sh never invokes the binary; it writes
#                     ~/.docker/config.json itself from base64. Checked, not assumed.
#                     Upstream ships the full docker-ce + containerd stack; nothing here uses it,
#                     and //:push goes through rules_oci, which speaks the registry API directly.
#   kubectl           Also absent upstream, which is WHY buildbuddy_setup_fixture_env.sh takes
#                     its Path 2 (pre-set BuildBuddy secrets) rather than reading the
#                     srql-fixtures secrets live. Adding kubectl here would silently switch CI
#                     to Path 1 and change the DSNs it receives -- see
#                     //rust/integration-db/README.md before doing that.
#   systemd, dbus, apparmor, iptables/nftables, polkit, packagekit, software-properties
#                     Upstream inherits these from a general-purpose Ubuntu image. A container
#                     that runs one build and exits has no use for an init system or a firewall.
#                     This is where most of the size difference comes from, and none of it is
#                     reachable from a workflow step.
#
# DERIVED BY DIFFING. The contents above and below came from comparing
# `dpkg-query -W` and the PATH of gcr.io/flame-public/rbe-ubuntu24-04 against this image
# (307 packages vs 117). An earlier revision of this file listed python3 here as unused; three
# genrules and //scripts/sign-oci-publish.sh use it, and CI failed on the first run with
# `/usr/bin/env: 'python3': No such file or directory`. Re-run that diff before removing
# anything, and prefer leaving a package in over reasoning about whether a step reaches it.
#
# Pinned by digest: a floating `24.04` tag would rebuild this image into a different base
# without the tag changing, and the glibc version is the thing that must not move.
FROM registry.carverauto.dev/mirror/library/ubuntu:24.04@sha256:d1e2e92c075e5ca139d51a140fff46f84315c0fdce203eab2807c7e495eff4f9

# ca-certificates  HTTPS to the BuildBuddy app, Harbor, and every Bazel repository fetch
# git              the ci_runner clones the repository into the workspace
# openssl          buildbuddy_setup_fixture_env.sh reads the fixture CA's expiry
#
# python3          REQUIRED, and the reason this file has a second revision. Reached at least
#                  three ways: `python3` in the genrule cmds of //docker/images/cnpg_image.bzl
#                  and release_images.bzl, //scripts/sign-oci-publish.sh, and the
#                  `#!/usr/bin/env python3` launcher of the py_binary targets in
#                  //go/pkg/models, //build/native_addons and //build/wasm_plugins. The
#                  hermetic 3.11 toolchain in MODULE.bazel does NOT cover these -- it serves
#                  py_binary rule execution, not a shebang or a shell-invoked `python3`.
# patch            http_archive falls back to the system binary for any patch its native
#                  implementation will not take.
# make             REQUIRED since v1.0.24.5 (issue 4855). Tool builds shell out to `make`; a
#                  hosted-Bazel dispatch that found it missing ran `apt-get install make` AS
#                  ROOT and wrote root-owned entries into the caches the workflow runners share
#                  on /bazel-cache, which then failed every uid-1001 runner with Permission
#                  denied. Ship the binary instead of letting a run escalate to install it
#                  (//docs/agent-runbooks.md, "Hosted-Bazel dispatches").
# curl, wget       repository rules that shell out instead of using Bazel's downloader.
# unzip, zip,      likewise for archive handling; Bazel extracts most formats itself but
# xz-utils, bzip2  several rule sets exec these directly.
# file             used by rules that sniff artifact types.
# netbase          /etc/services and /etc/protocols. Absent, some libc lookups fail in ways
#                  that surface as an unrelated network error.
# tzdata           timezone database. Without it everything is UTC, which silently changes the
#                  result of any timestamp test rather than failing loudly.
#
# --no-install-recommends is what keeps this small: git's recommends alone pull in perl-doc,
# less and a mail transport agent.
#
# NOT build-essential. gcc/g++/binutils is the single largest thing upstream has that this does
# not. Database callers use --strategy=TestRunner=local, which runs already-built test binaries
# here while compilation and linking remain eligible for RBE under --config=ci. The remote C
# toolchain lives in the executor image, not this runner. If a genuine runner-local link ever
# fails with "linker `cc` not found", add build-essential -- that is the fix, and it costs
# ~80 MB. `make` itself ships (v1.0.24.5): it is reached by name rather than through the
# compiler, and the apt-get-as-root workaround for its absence is what poisoned the shared
# caches in issue 4855.
RUN apt-get update \
 && DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive apt-get install -y --no-install-recommends \
      bzip2 \
      ca-certificates \
      curl \
      file \
      git \
      make \
      netbase \
      openssl \
      patch \
      python3 \
      tzdata \
      unzip \
      wget \
      xz-utils \
      zip \
 && rm -rf /var/lib/apt/lists/*

RUN groupadd --gid 1001 buildbuddy \
 && useradd --uid 1001 --gid 1001 --create-home --shell /bin/sh buildbuddy

# Matches the upstream image. BuildBuddy overrides both, but a shell is what a bare
# `docker run` against this image should land you in when debugging it by hand.
CMD ["/bin/sh", "-c", "bash"]
